# function callIt(object, a) { return object.m(a); }   function readIt(object) { return object.m; }   function storeIt(object, a) { object.m = a; }
# function callInTurns(object, times) { let sum = 0; for (let i = 0; i < times; i++) sum = object.m(sum); return sum; }
# `object.m(a)` is one call of the stub that reads the method and calls it, in a loop too. The site names the property and nothing else: no read by name stands in front of the call, and no call of a value behind it.
# These functions are written in no module, so no place of theirs remembers anything: the stubs that take a name answer. place-remembers-what-a-read-or-a-call-found.ir has the same functions with places.
# The stubs keep what they found last under the Structure of the receiver and the name. The shell asks each of them again behind every change that ends such an answer.
# The shell runs built-ins that are written in JavaScript. They are the engine's own here, and no stand-in answers for them.
